{k+1}{2}\\neq0\\).\n   Hence \\(m_i\\neq-1\\).  \n   \u2013 \\(m_i\\neq0\\) because the numerator \\(-i\\neq0\\).  \n   \u2013 The line is not vertical because it is written in slope\u2011intercept form.\n   Consequently each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n   The \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct: they have different\n   slopes (the map \\(i\\mapsto m_i\\) is injective).\n\n4. **Verification that the \\(L_i\\) cover \\(\\mathcal Q_{n,k}\\).**  \n   Fix a column \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le k\\).  \n   For a given \\(i\\) the intersection of \\(L_i\\) with the vertical line\n   \\(x=a\\) is obtained from (3):\n\n   \\[\n   y_i(a)=h+i+m_i(a-1)\n          =h+i-\\frac{i}{k+1-i}(a-1).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Multiplying (4) by the positive integer \\(k+1-i\\) gives\n\n   \\[\n   (k+1-i)\\,y_i(a)= (k+1-i)(h+i)-i(a-1).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   As \\(i\\) runs through \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\), the right\u2013hand side of (5) runs\n   through the arithmetic progression\n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\{\\,h(k+1)+(k+1-a),\\;h(k+1)+(k+1-a)+(k+1),\\dots ,\n          \\;h(k+1)+(k+1-a)+k(k+1)\\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Dividing by the varying factor \\(k+1-i\\) (which is a positive integer) we\n   obtain precisely the set\n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\{\\,h+ a,\\;h+ a+1,\\dots ,h+ k+1-a\\,\\bigr\\}\n   =\\{\\,b\\mid h+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality (6) shows that for the fixed column \\(a\\) the numbers\n   \\(\\{y_i(a)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\) are exactly the integers required in\n   \\(\\mathcal Q_{n,k}\\).  Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal Q_{n,k}\\) lies on a\n   unique line among \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n5. **Putting everything together.**  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{h}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\},\n   \\qquad h=n-k,\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(h+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\n   and it covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (the horizontal lines cover\n   the points with \\(b\