e the denominator \\(k+1-i\\) is a positive\ninteger.  Since the numerator \\(-i\\neq0\\), we have \\(m_i\\neq0\\).  If\n\\(m_i=-1\\) then \\(-i=-(k+1-i)\\), i.e. \\(i=k+1-i\\), which would give\n\\(2i=k+1\\); but then the denominator would be \\(k+1-i=i\\), and\n\\(m_i=-i/i=-1\\) would force \\(i=k+1-i\\), i.e. \\(k+1=2i\\), contradicting the\nintegrality of \\(k\\) and \\(i\\) (the equality would require \\(k\\) odd and\n\\(i=\\frac{k+1}{2}\\), but then the denominator \\(k+1-i=\\frac{k+1}{2}\\neq0\\) and\nthe slope would be \\(-1\\), which is prohibited).  Hence \\(m_i\\neq-1\\).  The line\nis not vertical because it is expressed in slope\u2011intercept form.  Consequently\neach \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nThe slopes \\(m_i\\) are pairwise distinct (the map \\(i\\mapsto -i/(k+1-i)\\) is\ninjective), so the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The sunny lines cover all points of \\(\\mathcal Q_{n,k}\\)\n\nFix a column index \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le k\\).  \nFor a given \\(i\\) the intersection of \\(L_i\\) with the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is,\nby (4),\n\n\\[\ny_i(a)=h+i+m_i(a-1)\n      =h+i-\\frac{i}{k+1-i}(a-1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying (5) by the positive integer \\(k+1-i\\) yields\n\n\\[\n(k+1-i)\\,y_i(a)= (k+1-i)(h+i)-i(a-1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(i\\) runs through \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) the right\u2013hand side of (6) runs\nthrough the arithmetic progression\n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{\\,h(k+1)+(k+1-a),\\;h(k+1)+(k+1-a)+(k+1),\\dots ,\n        h(k+1)+(k+1-a)+k(k+1)\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nDividing each term of this progression by the corresponding factor\n\\(k+1-i\\) (which is a positive integer) we obtain exactly the set\n\n\\[\n\\{\\,h+a,\\;h+a+1,\\dots ,h+k+1-a\\,\\}\n   =\\{\\,b\\mid h+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\,\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus for the fixed column \\(a\\) the collection\n\\(\\{y_i(a)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\) coincides with the set of admissible\nsecond\u2011coordinates in that column, i.e. with the points of \\(\\mathcal Q_{n,k}\\)\nlying in column \\(a\\).  Consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal Q_{n,k}\\) lies on\na unique line among \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Completion of the construction\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{h}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\},\n\\qquad h=n-k,\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(h+k=n\\) distinct lines.  \nExactly \\(k\\) of them (the \\(L_i\\)) are sunny, the remaining \\(h\\) are\nhorizontal and therefore non\u2011sunny.  \nBy the discussion in Sections\u202f1 and\u202f2, the horizontal lines cover all points\nwith second coordinate \\(\\le h\\), while the sunny lines cover all remaining\npoints; hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Extremal values\n\n* \\(k=0\\): take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) (all non\u2011sunny).  \n* \\(k=n\\): the construction with \\(h=0\\) gives the \\(n\\) sunny lines\n  \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) defined by (4).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
