ts are\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal Q_{n,k}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\;h+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y-(h+i)=m_i\\bigl(x-1\\bigr),\\qquad \n   m_i=-\\frac{i}{k+1-i}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Why each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.*  \n   The denominator \\(k+1-i\\) is a positive integer, so the slope is finite.\n   Because the numerator \\(-i\\neq0\\), \\(m_i\\neq0\\); and \\(m_i=-1\\) would give\n   \\(-i=-(k+1-i)\\), i.e. \\(2i=k+1\\), which is impossible for integer\n   \\(k\\ge3\\) (the left‑hand side is even, the right‑hand side odd).  Hence\n   \\(m_i\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\).\n\n   *The lines are distinct.*  The map \\(i\\mapsto m_i\\) is strictly\n   decreasing, therefore the slopes are pairwise different.\n\n   *Coverage of \\(\\mathcal Q_{n,k}\\).*  \n   Fix a column \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) in (5) gives\n   \\[\n   y_i(a)=h+i-\\frac{i}{k+1-i}(a-1)\n   \\qquad (1\\le i\\le k).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   Multiplying (6) by the positive integer \\(k+1-i\\) yields\n   \\[\n   (k+1-i)\\,y_i(a)= (k+1-i)(h+i)-i(a-1).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   As \\(i\\) runs from \\(1\\) to \\(k\\), the right‑hand side of (7) runs through\n   the arithmetic progression\n   \\[\n   h(k+1)+(k+1-a),\\;h(k+1)+(k+1-a)+(k+1),\\dots ,\n   h(k+1)+(k+1-a)+k(k+1).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   Dividing each term of (8) by the corresponding factor \\(k+1-i\\) (which\n   varies from \\(k\\) down to \\(1\\)) produces exactly the integer set\n   \\[\n   \\{\\,h+a,\\;h+a+1,\\dots ,h+k+1-a\\,\\}\n   =\\{\\,b\\mid h+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   Equation (9) is precisely the set of admissible second coordinates in\n   column \\(a\\) described by (4).  Consequently, for every column \\(a\\) the\n   numbers \\(\\{y_i(a)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\) coincide with the required\n   integers \\(b\\).  Hence each point of \\(\\mathcal Q_{n,k}\\) lies on a unique\n   line among \