\\[\nk=2\\quad\\text{is impossible}.                               \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\n#### 2.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the line\n\\(x=a\\).\n\n#### 2.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nPut \\(h=n-1\\) and use the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=h .                                      \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll points with \\(b\\le h\\) are covered.  The only uncovered point is\n\\((1,n)\\).  The line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny=-(n-1)x+n+1,\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nwhose slope \\(-(n-1)\\) is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); therefore it is\nsunny.  Thus a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists.\n\n#### 2.3  \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nh=n-k\\qquad (0\\le h\\le n-3)\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nand introduce the horizontal (non‑sunny) lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,h).                              \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nAfter (9) the uncovered points are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_{n,k}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\;h+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.           \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y-(h+i)=m_i\\bigl(x-1\\bigr),\\qquad \nm_i=-\\frac{i}{k+1-i}.                                         \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*Sunnyness.*  The denominator \\(k+1-i\\) is a positive integer, so the slope\nis finite; it is non‑zero because the numerator \\(-i\\neq0\\); and\n\\(m_i=-1\\) would imply \\(2i=k+1\\), impossible for integer \\(k\\ge3\\).\nHence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, so the lines are\npairwise different.\n\n*Coverage.*  Fix a column \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) in\n(11) gives  \n\n\\[\ny_i(a)=h+i-\\frac{i}{k+1-i}(a-1)\\qquad (1\\le i\\le k).          \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying (1