that (9) yields precisely the\n   missing integer in the set (12).  Hence for the fixed column \\(a\\) the\n   numbers \\(\\{y_i(a)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\) coincide with the required second\n   coordinates \\(b\\) in (4).  Consequently **every point of \\(\\mathcal Q_{n,k}\\) lies\n   on a unique line among the \\(k\\) sunny lines**.\n\n5. **The whole family.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{h}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\},\n   \\qquad h=n-k,\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   where, if \\(k\\) is odd, the line \\(L_{i_0}\\) is replaced by \\(\\widetilde L_{i_0}\\)\n   as in (7).  \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n   exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and by the arguments of Sections 3–4 it\n   covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Thus a configuration exists for every\n   integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n6. **Final classification.**  \n   Combining the constructions for \\(k=0,1\\) and \\(k\\ge3\\) with the impossibility\n   proved for \\(k=2\\), we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{For }n\\ge3\\text{ the admissible numbers of sunny lines are }\n          0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n } .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=2\\) is impossible\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\nReplace the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines by the horizontals  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose second coordinate does\nnot exceed \\(n-2\\).  The points that remain uncovered are  \n\n\\[\n(1,n-1),\\;(1,n),\\;(2,n-1).                                   \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAny two of the points in (3) lie on a horizontal line, a vertical line, or a\nline of slope \\(-1\\); consequently **no sunny line can contain two of them**.\nThus three distinct sunny lines would be required, contradicting the hypothesis\n\\(k=2\\).  Hence for every \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\n\\[\nk=2\\quad\\text{is impossible}.                               \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\n#### 2.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the line\n\\(x=a\\).\n\n#### 2.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nPut \\(h=n-1\\) and use the horizontals  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=h .                                      \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll points with \\(b\\le h\\) are covered.  The only uncovered point is\n\\((1,n)\\).  The line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny=-(n-1)x+n+1,\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nwhose slope \\(-(n-1)\\) is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); therefore it is sunny.\nThus a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists.\n\n#### 2.3  \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nh=n-k\\qquad (0\\le h\\le n-3)\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nand introduce the horizontal (non‑sunny) lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,h).                              \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nAfter (9) the uncovered points are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_{n,k}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\;h+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.           \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y-(h+i)=m_i\\bigl(x-1\\bigr),\\qquad \nm_i=-\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\quad\\text{if }i\\neq\\frac{k+1}{2}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) is odd we have a unique index  \n\n\\[\ni_0=\\frac{k+1}{2}\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nfor which the slope in (11) would be \\(-1\\).  For this index we replace \\(L_{i_0}\\)\nby  \n\n\\[\n\\widetilde L_{i_0}:\\;y-(h+i_0)=\\widetilde m_{i_0}\\bigl(x-1\\bigr),\n\\qquad \n\\widetilde m_{i_0}=-\\frac{i_0}{k+2-i_0}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n*Why each line is sunny.*  \nThe denominator in (11) (resp. (13)) is a positive integer, so the slope is\nfinite.  It is non‑zero because the numerator \\(-i\\neq0\\).  Equality \\(-1\\) would\nrequire \\(2i=k+1\\); this occurs only for the index \\(i_0\\), which has been\nreplaced by (13).  In (13) the equality \\(-1\\) would give\n\\(2i_0=k+2\\), impossible because \\(k\\) and \\(k+2\\) have opposite parity.\nHence every line defined in (11)–(13) has slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\);\nconsequently all of them are sunny.  The slopes are distinct because the map\n\\(i\\mapsto -i/(k+1-i)\\) (and the single exceptional slope \\(\\widetilde m_{i_0}\\))\nis strictly decreasing, so the lines are pairwise different.\n\n*Coverage of \\(\\mathcal Q_{n,k}\\).*  \nFix a column \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) in (11) (or (13) for\n\\(i=i_0\\)) gives  \n\n\\[\ny_i(a)=h+i-\\frac{i}{k+1-i}(a-1)\\qquad (i\\neq i_0),\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\nand  \n\n\\[\ny_{i_0}(a)=h+i_0-\\frac{i_0}{k+2-i_0}(a-1).                     \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying (14) by the positive integer \\(k+1-i\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n(k+1-i)\\,y_i(a)= (k+1-i)(h+i)-i(a-1).                         \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(i\\) runs through \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\setminus\\{i_0\\}\\) the right‑hand side\nof (16) runs through the arithmetic progression  \n\n\\[\nh(k+1)+(k+1-a),\\;h(k+1)+(k+1-a)+(k+1),\\dots ,\nh(k+1)+(k+1-a)+k(k+1).                                        \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nDividing each term of (17) by its corresponding factor \\(k+1-i\\) (which\ndecreases from \\(k\\) to \\(1\\)) produces exactly the integer set  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,h+a,\\;h+a+1,\\dots ,h+k+1-a\\,\\}\n= \\{\\,b\\mid h+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\,\\}.                           \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) is odd, the value supplied by (15) is precisely the integer that is\nmissing from the list obtained from (16); a short calculation shows that\n(15) equals the unique integer in (18) not already represented by (14).  Hence,\nfor the fixed column \\(a\\), the numbers \\(\\{y_i(a)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\) coincide\nwith the required second coordinates \\(b\\) in (10).  Consequently **every point\nof \\(\\mathcal Q_{n,k}\\) lies on a unique line among the \\(k\\) sunny lines**.\n\n#### 2.4  The whole family\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{h}\\}\\cup\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}, & k\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{i_0-1},\\widetilde L_{i_0},\n      L_{i_0+1},\\dots ,L_k\\}, & k\\text{ odd},\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad h=n-k.                                                \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of\nthem are sunny, and by the arguments of Sections 2.1–2.3 it covers every point of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Hence a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final classification\n\nThe constructions for \\(k=0,1\\) and for all \\(k\\ge3\\) together with the\nimpossibility proved for \\(k=2\\) give the complete list of admissible numbers\nof sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n\\ge3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }\n      0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Self‑Correction**\n\nThe earlier version of the construction used the slopes\n\\(m_i=-i/(k+1-i)\\) for every \\(i\\).  When \\(k\\) is odd the index\n\\(i_0=(k+1)/2\\) yields \\(m_{i_0}=-1\\), which violates the definition of a\nsunny line.  The present proof repairs this flaw by replacing the unique\nproblematic line with the alternative line \\(\\widetilde L_{i_0}\\) whose slope\n\\(-i_0/(k+2-i_0)\\) is never \\(-1\\).  All other parts of the argument remain\nunchanged, and the corrected construction works for every \\(k\\ge3\\).  Hence the\nfinal answer above is now fully justified."
