e can contain **at most one** point of \\(P\\).  \nSince we have only two sunny lines, at most two points of \\(P\\) can be covered, contradicting (3).  \nThus no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nEvery line is non‑sunny, and a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny.  The only point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) not lying on one of these lines is \\((1,n)\\).  \nThe line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny = -(n-1)x + 2n-1 .\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(-(n-1)\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore the line is sunny.  \nThus we have exactly one sunny line, proving that \\(k=1\\) is possible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for every \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nh=n-k .\n\\]\n\n#### 4.1  The non‑sunny part  \n\nTake the \\(h\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=h .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are non‑sunny.  After removing them, the points that are still uncovered form the set  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(i,j)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le j\\le k+1-i\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically, \\(T_k\\) is a right‑angled integer triangle of side length \\(k\\).\n\n#### 4.2  The sunny part  \n\nFor each integer \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_r:\\; y = -\\frac{r}{\\,k+1-r\\,}\\,x + (k+1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*The slope* of \\(L_r\\) equals \\(-\\dfrac{r}{k+1-r}\\); because \\(1\\le r\\le k\\) the denominator is a positive integer, and the fraction is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Hence every \\(L_r\\) is sunny.\n\n*Every point of \\(T_k\\) lies on one of the \\(L_r\\).*  \nLet \\((i,j)\\in T_k\\).  Since \\(i+j\\le k+1\\) we can write  \n\n\\[\nj = (k+1)-i - s\\qquad\\text{with }s\\ge 0 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nChoose \\(r=i\\).  Substituting \\(x=i\\) into (6) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,i + (k+1)\n   = (k+1)-\\frac{i^{2}}{k+1-i}\n   = (k+1)-i-\\frac{i(i-1)}{k+1-i}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i\\) and \\(k+1-i\\) are consecutive integers in the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\), the integer \\(i(i-1)\\) is divisible by \\(k+1-i\\); consequently the last term equals an integer \\(s\\ge 0\\).  Using (7) we obtain \\(y=j\\).  Hence \\((i,j)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\) and therefore belongs to \\(L_i\\).\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nIf \\(r\\neq s\\) then the slopes \\(-\\dfrac{r}{k+1-r}\\) and \\(-\\dfrac{s}{k+1-s}\\) are different, so the lines \\(L_r\\) and \\(L_s\\) are distinct.\n\nThus the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny, distinct, and together cover the whole set \\(T_k\\).\n\n#### 4.3  Putting the two parts together  \n\nThe family consisting of the \\(h=n-k\\) vertical lines (4) together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines (6) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of them (the points with first coordinate \\(\\le h\\) are covered by the vertical lines, the remaining points belong to \\(T_k\\) and are covered by the sunny lines).  \n\nHence a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\n* \\(k=0\\) – always possible (vertical lines).  \n* \\(k=1\\) – always possible (all horizontals plus one suitable diagonal).  \n* \\(k=2\\) – impossible (proved in §1).  \n* \\(k\\ge 3\\) – possible f