ose coordinates sum to the same\n   constant; any line that contains **two** points of a diagonal must itself be a\n   diagonal (slope \\(-1\\)) and hence is non‑sunny.  Therefore each sunny line can\n   meet a given diagonal in **at most one** point.  \n\n   The diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) contains the \\(n\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   T=\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because a sunny line meets \\(D_{n+1}\\) in at most one point, the two sunny\n   lines together can cover at most two points of \\(T\\).  The remaining\n   \\(n-2\\ge 1\\) points of \\(T\\) would have to be covered by the other\n   \\(n-2\\) diagonal lines, but none of those diagonals contains a point of\n   \\(T\\).  Hence a covering with exactly two sunny lines is impossible for every\n   \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n3. **Constructions for the remaining values of \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{i}:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   Every required point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\); all lines are\n   non‑sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_{j}:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n   \\]\n\n   together with the sunny line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\),\n\n   \\[\n   S:\\;y=-(n-1)x+2n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The horizontal lines cover all points except \\((1,n)\\); the line \\(S\\) covers\n   that point and is sunny (its slope \\(-(n-1)\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).\n\n   *\\(3\\le k\\le n\\).*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   r=n-k\\qquad (0\\le r\\le n-3).\n   \\]\n\n   Use the \\(r\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{i}:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,r)\n   \\]\n\n   (all non‑sunny).  \n   The points that are still uncovered are exactly those with abscissa\n   \\(a\\in\\{r+1,r+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); for a fixed \\(a\\) there are\n   \\(n+1-a\\) admissible ordinates.  Hence the uncovered set is the right‑angled\n   triangle\n\n   \\[\n   T_{k}=\\bigl\\{(r+i,j)\\;:\\;1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le j\\le k+1-i\\bigr\