maining \\(n-3\\) non‑sunny lines can each cover at most one of the still uncovered points of \\(D_{n+1}\\), but there are \\(n-2\\) such points – a contradiction.  \n   Hence no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n3. **Construction for the remaining values of \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n     Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n     \\[\n     x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n     \\]\n\n     Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\); all lines are non‑sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n     Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with any sunny line passing through the uncovered point \\((n,1)\\) (for instance the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\)).  \n     Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k\\ge 3\\).*  \n     Write  \n\n     \\[\n     r=n-k\\qquad(0\\le r\\le n-3).\n     \\]\n\n     – **Non‑sunny part.**  \n       Take the \\(r\\) vertical lines  \n\n       \\[\n       x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=r .\n       \\]\n\n       They cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose abscissa does not exceed \\(r\\).\n\n     – **Sunny part.**  \n       The points that are still uncovered form the right‑angled triangle  \n\n       \\[\n       T_{k}= \\bigl\\{(r+i,\\;j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le j\\le k+1-i\\bigr\\}.\n       \\tag{1}\n       \\]\n\n       For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n       \\[\n       L_{t}:\\; y = x-(t-1).\n       \\tag{2}\n       \\]\n\n       The slope of \\(L_{t}\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore every \\(L_{t}\\) is sunny\n       (its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n       Substituting \\(x=r+i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le k\\)) into (2) gives  \n\n       \\[\n       y = r+i-(t-1)=i-t+1 .\n       \\tag{3}\n       \\]\n\n       Hence \\(L_{t}\\) contains precisely the points  \n\n       \\[\n       \\bigl\\{(r+i,\\;i-t+1)\\mid i=t,t+1,\\dots ,k\\bigr\\}.\n       \\tag{4}\n       \\]\n\n       For a fixed \\(t\\) the ordinate in (4) runs through the integers\n       \\(1,2,\\dots ,k+1-t\\); consequently the set (4) is exactly the subset of\n       \\(T_{k}\\) whose second coordinate is at least \\(t\\).  \n       The sets (4) for different values of \\(t\\) are pairwise disjoint and\n       their union equals \\(T_{k}\\).  Thus the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\)\n       cover all points of the triangle \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n     The total number of lines is \\(r+k=n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them (the \\(L_{t}\\)’s)\n     are sunny.  This construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  \n     The case \\(k=n\\) corresponds to \\(r=0\\); the construction above reduces to the\n     family \\(\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\), i.e. \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines of slope\n     \\(1\\).\n\nSince we have exhibited configurations for all admissible values of \\(k\\) and have proved that \\(k=2\\) can never occur, the description of the set of possible \\(k\\) is complete.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All }k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ are possible; }k=2\\text{ is impossible}.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
