mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\((n-k)+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(S_n\\) (the large diagonals by the forbidden lines, the small diagonals by the sunny lines).  \n\n   Therefore a configuration exists for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nCombining the necessary condition (1) with the explicit construction above, we obtain the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the *diagonal*\n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|D_s|=s-1\\); the diagonals form a partition of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary condition on the number of sunny lines\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nIf a line contains two distinct points of the same diagonal \\(D_s\\), then those two points determine the line \\(x+y=s\\), whose slope is \\(-1\\); consequently such a line is *not* sunny.  \n\nHence a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of any diagonal \\(D_s\\).\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nIf a diagonal \\(D_s\\) has size larger than \\(k\\) (i.e. \\(s-1>k\\)), then at most \\(k\\) of its points can be covered by sunny lines, so at least one point of \\(D_s\\) must be covered by a non‑sunny line. Consequently **every diagonal with size \\(>k\\) must appear as a non‑sunny line in \\(\\mathcal L\\).**\n\nThe diagonals whose sizes exceed \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nD_{k+2},D_{k+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1},\n\\]\n\nand there are \\(n-k\\) of them. Therefore any admissible family must contain at least \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines. This yields the necessary condition\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any admissible family must contain at least }n-k\\text{ forbidden lines}.}\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction showing the condition is sufficient\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall exhibit a family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines,\n* covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The non‑sunny part\n\nFor each \\(s=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\) take the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nThese are the diagonals of sizes \\(k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n\\); they are pairwise distinct and none of them is sunny.  \nTogether they cover all points belonging to the *large* diagonals \\(D_{k+2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The sunny part\n\nConsider the \\(k\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=x-i+1\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Every small diagonal is hit by exactly one sunny line\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in D_t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le k+1\\); thus \\(a+b=t\\) and \\(1\\le a\\le t-1\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\ni:=a-b+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a+b=t\\),\n\n\\[\ni = a-(t-a)+1 = 2a-t+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le a\\le t-1\\) we have \\(1\\le i\\le k\\). Moreover\n\n\\[\nb = a-i+1 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the equation of the line \\(L_i\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).\n\nConversely, if a point \\((a,b)\\) lies on some \\(L_i\\) then \\(b=a-i+1\