n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad \n   L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\;( \\text{through }(1,2),(3,1)),\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=-2x+5\\;( \\text{through }(2,1),(1,3)).\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(k\\ge4\\).*  \n   Start from the construction for \\(k=3\\).  \n   Replace any of the vertical lines \\(x=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n-3\\)) by a sunny line through \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,2)\\) (slope 1).  \n   Each replacement increases the number of sunny lines by one while keeping all points covered.  \n   Repeating this we obtain configurations with exactly any prescribed \\(k\\) satisfying \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   The case \\(k=n\\) is obtained by taking \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, each passing through a different point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n\n   Assume a covering with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \n   Let the non‑sunny lines consist of \\(r\\) horizontal lines, \\(c\\) vertical lines and \\(d\\) diagonals; then  \n\n   \\[\n   r+c+d=n-2. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Denote by  \n\n   \\[\n   R'=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\text{selected rows}\\},\\qquad \n   C'=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\text{selected columns}\\},\\qquad \n   D'=\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{\\text{selected diagonals}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Points not covered by any non‑sunny line are  \n\n   \\[\n   U=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\in C',\\;b\\in R',\\;a+b\\in D'\\}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because of (1) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   |C'|+|R'|=n+d+2\\ge n+2,\n   \\qquad |C'|,|R'|\\ge2. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Common difference.*  \n   A set of \\(m\\) integers contains at least \\(m-1\\) distinct positive differences.  \n   Hence the sets of positive differences  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta_C=\\{c_2-c_1\\mid c_1,c_2\\in C',c_2>c_1\\},\n   \\qquad \n   \\Delta_R=\\{r_1-r_2\\mid r_1,r_2\\in R',r_1>r_2\\}\n   \\]\n\n   satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\Delta_C|+|\\Delta_R|\\ge(|C'|-1)+(|R'|-1)=|C'|+|R'|-2\\ge n .\n   \\]\n\n   Since the possible positive differences are the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-