ber of sunny lines one by one.\n   For \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k-3\\) replace the vertical line \\(x=j\\) by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\;y=x-j+1\\qquad\\text{(i.e. the line through }(j,1)\\text{ and }(j+1,2)).\n   \\]\n\n   After the replacement of the first \\(t\\) vertical lines (\\(1\\le t\\le k-3\\)) the set of lines consists of  \n\n   * the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_t\\);\n   * the still‑present vertical lines \\(x=t+1,\\dots ,n-3\\);\n   * the three sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) covering the copy of \\(T_3\\).\n\n   **Why no point is lost.**  \n   Consider a point \\((i,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le t\\).  \n   If \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(S_i\\).  \n   If \\(b\\ge2\\) write \\(b=1+r\\) with \\(r\\ge1\\).  \n   Then \\((i,b)=(i+r,1+r)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(S_{i+r-1}\\) (because \\(S_{i+r-1}\\) contains all points \\((i+r-1+s,1+s)\\) for \\(s\\ge0\\)).  \n   Since \\(i+r-1\\le t\\) (otherwise \\(i+b>n+1\\) would contradict \\((i,b)\\in T_n\\)), the required line \\(S_{i+r-1}\\) is present.  \n   Hence every point of the columns that have been “removed’’ is still covered.  \n   The points in the untouched columns \\(x=t+1,\\dots ,n-3\\) are covered by the remaining vertical lines, and the points of the right‑hand triangle are covered by \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).  \n   Consequently after the \\(k-3\\) replacements we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n\n   Assume a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines contains exactly two sunny lines.  \n   Let the non‑sunny lines consist of  \n\n   * \\(r\\) horizontal lines,\n   * \\(c\\) vertical lines,\n   * \\(d\\) lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   r+c+d=n-2\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Denote by  \n\n   \\[\n   R=\\{\\,\\text{rows used by the horizontal lines}\\,\\},\\qquad\n   C=\\{\\,\\text{columns used by the vertical lines}\\,\\},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   D=\\{\\,\\text{constants }s\\text{ of the diagonals