"**Final Verdict:** The solution is **invalid** because it contains several **Critical Errors** that break the logical chain of the proof.\n\n---\n\n### List of Findings\n* **Location:** \u201cThe three sunny lines \u2026  \n  \\(L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{n+1}2\\) \u2026\u201d  \n  **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 the given line does **not** pass through the required points \\((n-2,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\); the correct constant should be \\(\\tfrac{n+2}{2}\\). Consequently the claimed covering for \\(k=3\\) (when \\(n\\ge4\\)) is false.\n\n* **Location:** \u201cFrom (1) one obtains \\(|C|+|R|\\ge n+2\\).\u201d (section\u202f3, impossibility of \\(k=2\\))  \n  **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 with \\(r+c+d=n-2\\) we have \\(|C|+|R|=c+r=n-2-d\\le n-2\\), never \\(\\ge n+2\\). The subsequent argument relies on this false inequality.\n\n* **Location:** \u201cIf no vertical line is present \u2026 each point of the first column \u2026 must lie on a distinct line \u2026\u201d (section\u202f4, impossibility of \\(k\\ge4\\))  \n  **Issue:** Justification Gap \u2013 the argument assumes that the only possible vertical line is \\(x=1\\). It ignores the possibility of additional vertical non\u2011sunny lines (e.g. \\(x=2\\)), which would allow several points of the second column to share a line. The conclusion that every remaining point of the second column needs a distinct line is therefore unjustified.\n\n* **Location:** \u201cNo non\u2011vertical line can contain two of its points, so each of the remaining \\(n-1\\) points must lie on a different line.\u201d (same section)  \n  **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 the statement is false if another vertical line (a non\u2011sunny line) is present; the proof does not rule out this case, so the bound on the number of points that can be covered outside the first two columns is incorrect.\n\n* **Location:** Overall conclusion that only \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\) are possible.  \n  **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 because the impossibility proofs for \\(k=2\\) and \\(k\\ge4\\) are invalid, the final classification is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n##"
