"Summary**\n\n*Verdict.*  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn particular  \n\n* \\(k=0\\) (all lines non\u2011sunny) is always attainable;  \n* \\(k=1\\) is always attainable;  \n* \\(k=3\\) is always attainable;  \n* no configuration with \\(k\\ge 4\\) sunny lines exists, and \\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\).\n\n*Method sketch.*  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *Non\u2011sunny* lines are those parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); all other lines are *sunny*.  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Existence.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Use the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); each is non\u2011sunny and together they contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Take the vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) (all non\u2011sunny).  \n   The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); cover it by the sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) (slope \\(-\\frac1{n-1}\\)).  \n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   \u2013 If \\(n=3\\) the six points of \\(T_3\\) are covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5 .\n   \\]\n\n   \u2013 If \\(n\\ge4\\) use the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3\n   \\]\n\n   (all non\u2011sunny).  \n   The points that remain are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. a translated copy of \\(T_3\\).  \n   They are covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1&:\\;y=x-(n-3)      &&\\text{through }(n-2,1),(n-1,2),\\\\\n   L_2&:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{n+1}2 &&\\text{through }(n-2,2),(n,1),\\\\\n   L_3&:\\;y=-2x+2n-1    &&\\text{through }(n-2,3),(n-1,1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   All three slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the lines are sunny.  \n   Together with the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, three of which are sunny.\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Suppose a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines contains exactly two sunny lines.  \n   Let the non\u2011sunny lines consist of \\(r\\) horizontals, \\(c\\) verticals and \\(d\\) diagonals (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \n   Then  \n\n   \\[\n   r+c+d=n-2\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   and the points not hit by any non\u2011sunny line are  \n\n   \\[\n   U=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\notin C,\\;b\\notin R,\\;a+b\\notin D\\},\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(C,R,D\\) are the sets of columns, rows and diagonal\u2011constants used by the non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   From (1) one obtains \\(|C|+|R|\\ge n+2\\).  \n   Consequently the sets of positive differences \\(\\Delta_C,\\Delta_R\\) each contain at least \\(|C|-1\\) and \\(|R|-1\\) elements, so  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\Delta_C|+|\\Delta_R|\\ge n .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since the possible differences are the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\), the two difference sets intersect; choose a common difference \\(\\delta\\).  \n   Because \\(|D|\\le n-2\\), there exist two distinct diagonal\u2011constants \\(s_1<s_2\\) not belonging to \\(D\\); set \\(\\delta'=s_2-s_1\\).  \n   Using \\(\\delta\\in\\Delta_C\\cap\\Delta_R\\) we find  \n\n   \\[\n   a,a+\\delta\\in C,\\qquad b,b-\\delta\\in R .\n   \\]\n\n   The three points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_1=(a,b),\\;P_2=(a+\\delta,b),\\;P_3=(a+\\delta,b-\\delta)\n   \\]\n\n   belong to \\(U\\) and lie pairwise on a horizontal, a vertical and a diagonal line, respectively.  \n   Hence any line containing two of them is non\u2011sunny, so a sunny line can contain at most one of the three points.  \n   With only two sunny lines we could cover at most two points of \\(U\\), contradicting the fact that all three points of \\(U\\) must be covered.  \n   Therefore a covering with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge4\\).**  \n   Assume a covering with at least four sunny lines exists.  \n   Let the non\u2011sunny lines be \\(m=n-k\\le n-4\\) in number.\n\n   *If no vertical line is present*, every point of the first column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid1\\le b\\le n\\}\\) must lie on a distinct line, because any line containing two of them would be vertical (non\u2011sunny).  \n   Hence we would need at least \\(n\\) sunny lines, contradicting \\(k\\le n-4\\).\n\n   *Hence a vertical line must be present.*  \n   Without loss of generality it is \\(x=1\\); it covers the whole first column.  \n   Consider the second column \\(\\{(2,b)\\mid1\\le b\\le n-1\\}\\).  \n   No non\u2011vertical line can contain two of its points, so each of the remaining \\(n-1\\) points must lie on a different line.  \n   Consequently **all** the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines intersect the second column, i.e. each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines passes through exactly one point of this column.\n\n   Every sunny line already contains one point of the second column; it can therefore contribute at most one additional point (lying in a different column).  \n   Hence the total number of points that can be covered outside the first two columns is at most \\(k\\).\n\n   The number of points of \\(T_n\\) that are not in the first two columns equals  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|-n-(n-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-(2n-1)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge3\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2>k\\qquad\\text{whenever }k\\le n-4 .\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed \\((n-1)(n-2)>2(n-4)\\) holds for all \\(n\\ge3\\).)  \n   Thus the remaining points cannot all be covered \u2013 a contradiction.  \n   Therefore no configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only values of \\(k\\) that can occur are \\(0,1,3\\).  All three are realizable by the constructions of step\u202f2, and the impossibility of any other value follows from steps\u202f3 and\u202f4. \u220e"
